+The 1,922 photographs were nearly evenly divided between white-like (966) and pigmented (956) flowers. A one-standard-deviation increase in Temperature PC1—warmer maximum and warm-quarter conditions with greater growing-degree accumulation—was associated with 42% lower odds of pigmentation (posterior mean log-odds -0.542; odds ratio 0.58, 95% CrI 0.36–0.95). Among pigmented flowers, conditional visible intensity declined by 0.319 SD per SD of Temperature PC1 (95% CrI -0.484 to -0.156) and by 0.138 SD per SD of the slope–roughness–TRI axis (-0.231 to -0.045). The structural East/West adjustment also differed for intensity (East minus West -0.435 SD, -0.789 to -0.019), but it was not assigned an ecological mechanism. Precipitation, climatic seasonality, soil and shortwave-radiation intervals included zero after conditioning on the spatial field.
+
+Substantial residual geography remained. Posterior mean SPDE range was 132.7 km (95% CrI 88.7–195.6) for pigmentation state and 60.9 km (31.0–115.8) for conditional intensity. The overlapping intervals do not establish a formal difference, but the fitted scales were consistent with broader regional organization of state than of within-pigmented intensity. Geographically blocked prediction gave image-level AUC=0.863 for pigmentation state (cell any-pigmented AUC=0.858; majority-pigmented AUC=0.871; Brier=0.150). Conditional intensity had RMSE=0.919, MAE=0.715 and 95% predictive coverage=0.944 across 674 pigmented-support cells. The broad model therefore provided a transferable natural reference while retaining geography not uniquely attributable to measured environment.
 
